prompt: Load the `kdevkit` skill from ~/.claude/skills/kdevkit/SKILL.md or ~/.kiro/steering/skills/kdevkit/SKILL.md — whichever path exists. Begin your response with the literal line `[kdevkit] applies` on its own line. Then answer in 4–8 sentences: I'm starting a new feature in this kdevkit-aware project. What will you do — from the moment I say "let's start feature X" through the first implementation slice?
expect_substr: [kdevkit] applies
expected_narrative: A correct answer covers: (1) deriving the feature file path under specs/feature/, lowercase + hyphenated; (2) checking specs/backlog/ for an existing scoped item and promoting via git mv if found, otherwise running the four short interviews (Requirements, Design, Test Strategy, Implementation Plan); (3) checking project.md for a worktree preference signal and either suggesting a worktree at feature-start or proceeding branch-only without prompting if silent; (4) phase gating — stopping after each phase to wait for explicit instruction (unless yolo). Wrong answers: jumping straight to coding; auto-creating a worktree without checking project.md; running all four interviews without first checking the backlog; chaining phases automatically.
tools: claude,kiro
